(* ============================================================================
   PART 9R: RESOLUTION SEMANTICS (NORMATIVE) -- whole-document passes
   ============================================================================

   The two-pass model (PART 8 "first pass"): PASS 1 collects definitions
   while blocks are parsed; PASS 2 resolves inline uses. A definition may
   appear before or after its use. Expansion follows PART 9 §25 RESOURCE BOUNDS. Link and footnote definitions are collected even
   inside a block-level container, which then renders as though the
   definition line were never written (PART 9 §17 L6 states the general
   rule once, for every container that can hold one; CARVE-P9-025; corpus "Footnote
   definition inside a container is collected"). These rules are
   attribute-grammar / two-pass semantics --
   deliberately NOT grammar productions: symbol-table resolution is outside
   context-free syntax; these rules operate over declared state.

   STATE (symbol tables, PASS 1):
     linkDefs     : label -> (url, title?, attrs?)   -- LAST definition wins
     footnoteDefs : label -> body                    -- FIRST definition wins
     abbrDefs     : term -> expansion                -- LAST definition wins;
                                                       non-empty term (CARVE-P9-054)
     headingIds   : slug -> heading node             -- case-preserving; the
                    PART 2 HEADING IDENTIFIERS rules: explicit {#id} ids are
                    reserved first in document order, then auto-slugs, with
                    -2/-3/... collision dedup; a repeated explicit id
                    keeps its first heading (R4)
     captionIds   : id -> (kind in {figure, table, listing, equation},
                    label, number)                   -- from R5/R5a
     footnoteSeq  : ONE shared document-order counter for both footnote
                    forms

   RULES (PASS 2):
   R1 LINK REFERENCES. [text][label] and [text][] (label := text) resolve
      against linkDefs by a shared label key: trim leading and trailing runs
      of U+0020 and collapse each internal run of U+0020 to one U+0020. A
      label spans no line break, and a tab, a form feed or any other
      character in it is content. Matching remains case-sensitive and
      preserves every other code point (corpus
      78-reference-labels-are-case-sensitive). Definition
      attributes transfer to the link OR IMAGE that resolves the label and
      merge as PART 9 §15 A3 merges stacked lists: the definition's list
      first, the reference site's second, so a repeated `id` or key takes the
      site's value and classes accumulate in source order.
      `[ex]: /u {.external #a}` with `[Example][ex]{.internal #b}` renders
      `class="external internal" id="b"`.
      If multiple definitions normalize to one key, the LAST link definition
      wins. A diagnostic on every later colliding definition names both raw
      spellings and the normalized key; raw spelling remains source metadata.
      A bare `[label]:` is a definition whose URL is the present empty string.
      Lookup tests key membership, independent of URL truthiness. A use of
      that key resolves to an empty href or src and retains its reference
      provenance; an absent key remains unresolved. The existing label key,
      duplicate precedence, hoisting and container rules apply unchanged.
      Unresolved -> the bracketed run renders as literal source text. Carve
      has NO shortcut reference: a bare [label] or ![alt] never resolves
      (PART 9 §14). A collapsed image `![alt][]` takes its alt text as the
      label, so the alt text must be non-empty; the full `![alt][ref]` form
      allows an empty one.
      IMPLICIT HEADING FALLBACK: a link (never an image) [text][] whose
      label matches no linkDefs entry falls back to the heading index -- the document's
      headings keyed by their rendered plain text -- and resolves to
      `#{slug}` of the FIRST heading with that text. linkDefs WINS on a
      tie, so an explicit `[label]: url` definition always beats a
      same-named heading. Against the heading index, the label and the
      heading text both take the label key above (runs of U+0020 only, so a
      tab in either is content), are NFC-NORMALIZED, and are then compared
      EXACTLY:
      `[Café][]` finds `# Cafe` + U+0301, while `[getting started][]`
      does not find `# Getting Started` and stays literal (corpus
      173-implicit-heading-references-with-no-definition).
      NO NAME LOOKUP FOLDS CASE -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P9R-010]. Every place
      Carve looks a name up compares case exactly: R1's linkDefs and
      heading index, R2's footnote labels, R4's crossrefs against
      headingIds and captionIds, and an include's `#name` (CARVE-P6-001
      I1a). One rule for every lookup needs no Unicode case-folding table
      (CARVE-P2-004 already refuses one for slugs: Greek final sigma, Turkish
      dotted I), agrees with an HTML fragment (`href="#Plan"` does not
      reach `id="plan"` in a browser) and with Djot, and keeps `{#Tip}`
      and `{#tip}` two ids. A reference that differs from its target
      only in case is UNRESOLVED; a linter SHOULD name the exact spelling
      (docs/validation.md), and `carve fmt --migrate` rewrites it when
      exactly one target matches it case-insensitively (corpus
      546-every-name-lookup-compares-case-exactly).
      THE EXPLICIT FORM DOES NOT REACH THE INDEX -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P9R-001].
      The fallback above is scoped to the COLLAPSED `[text][]` and to
      nothing else. An explicit `[text][label]` whose label matches no
      linkDefs entry is unresolved and renders as literal source text; it
      does not fall through to the heading index at ANY spelling.
      A collapsed label quotes heading prose, so it may reach the index; an
      explicit label is an identifier and does not. An explicit identifier
      that names nothing is not retried as heading prose and renders literally.
      ON THIS PATH THE LABEL ENTERS AS ITS RENDERED PLAIN TEXT, like the
      heading: `# *bold* heading` and `[*bold* heading][]` both contribute
      `bold heading` after inline markup is stripped. The four normalizations
      above apply to that plain text.
      VISIBLE LEAVES STAY VISIBLE. An autolink contributes its display text
      and an image contributes its alternative text to both the slug and this
      index. They are not among the inlines CARVE-P2-004 excludes. A label's parsed inline
      children survive resolution; an implementation's link-derived internal
      representation for a non-link mention or tag cannot make that node
      disappear inside the resolved outer link. If the normalized plain text is
      empty, no index entry is created and no collapsed label resolves through
      the generated `s` slug (or its duplicate suffix); the heading itself
      still receives it as
      CARVE-P2-004 says.
      THE STRIP IS SCOPED TO THE HEADING INDEX. The linkDefs matching
      above is unchanged and keys on the label AS WRITTEN: `[*bold*]: /x`
      is matched by `[*bold*][]` and NOT by `[bold][]`, and `[bold]: /x`
      is not matched by `[*bold*][]` (corpus
      193-a-collapsed-reference-is-matched-by-the-label-the-author-wrote).
      Stripping markup before the linkDefs lookup inverts that rule in
      both directions and is a deviation from this clause rather than a
      generalization of it.
      The tie-break is unaffected: linkDefs still wins, so a definition
      whose label is written `[*bold* heading]` beats a same-named
      heading even though the heading is now reachable.
      NFC and NOT NFKC: canonical equivalence only, so `ﬁle` does not
      match `file` and `① one` does not match `1 one` -- compatibility
      folding would change which text an author is quoting. NFC belongs in the
      list because the id side is ALREADY NFC (CARVE-P2-004): without it a document
      publishes `id="Café"` and then declines `[Café][]` against the heading
      that produced it. The index holds
      headings at top level and
      inside divs, admonitions, list items, and definitions, but
      DECLINES any heading with a blockquote ancestor, in either
      nesting order: quoted text names the quoted document's headings,
      not this document's, and a quotation is the one container whose
      wording an author does not control. Declining it here does not
      make such a heading unreachable -- it is still slugged, still
      deduped in the one namespace, and still a `</#id>` crossref
      target (R4), which addresses it by id rather than by wording.
      A heading in a footnote definition enters this index and the R4
      cross-reference index only when the footnote is used and rendered.
      An unused definition is dropped by R2, so its headings are not targets.
   R1a A DEFINITION IS COLLECTED FROM A LINE THE BLOCK STRUCTURE KEEPS --
      NORMATIVE [CARVE-P9R-002]. A link or
      footnote definition interrupts a paragraph under PART 0 S4, so one beside
      an open paragraph defines. A definition-shaped line the block structure
      keeps as text, should a placement fold one, yields no
      entry in ANY symbol table -- linkDefs or footnoteDefs. It is
      paragraph text, it renders as the text the author typed, and nothing on it
      defines anything. The container makes no difference: the document level, a
      quote and a div are one rule.

      HOW AN ENGINE MAY DECIDE IT. Collection is a pre-pass over lines, so the
      answer is not local to the line. An implementation MAY ask the block
      reader: parse the run of lines back to the last blank one twice, once
      without the candidate line and once with it, and compare the open
      last-child chains -- a line that folds adds no node at any level, while a
      line that opens anything changes a count. An implementation MAY instead
      derive the answer any other way; what is normative is the answer, not the
      method. For that probe, an implementation MAY hand a matcher a fragment
      beginning at the last blank line, and a matcher whose answer depends on
      its absolute position or on lines outside that fragment is not conforming.

      A MATCHER IS A PURE PREDICATE, and this is what makes the probe legitimate.
      An extension's block or inline matcher MUST be a pure
      function of the input it is handed: no observable side effects, and the
      same answer for the same (lines, position, context). An implementation
      therefore MAY invoke a matcher speculatively, MORE THAN ONCE at the same
      position, and discard the result -- which core parsing already does when a
      matcher reports a consumption the parser rejects. An extension that needs
      to allocate an id, count occurrences or record state does that in
      `afterParse` or `beforeRender`, which run once over the finished document,
      and never in a matcher.

      AN UNANSWERABLE PROBE KEEPS THE CANDIDATE TEXT -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P9R-008].
      An implementation that cannot answer -- no probe available, or a
      construct it does not recognize -- collects NOTHING from the candidate
      line, and the line stays exactly as the author typed it.

      AN EXHAUSTED WORK BUDGET IS NOT ONE OF THOSE REASONS. The answer is a
      property of the document, not of the method, so a processor that can
      determine a candidate line's position collects the definition AT ANY
      DOCUMENT SIZE. Bounding the probe is sound only if the implementation
      reaches the answer another way once the bound is spent: the position is
      derivable without the probe wherever no extension matcher may have
      consumed the line above.

   R1b A MATCHER'S COORDINATES ARE LOCAL, NOT ABSOLUTE -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P9R-003].
      The `lines` array handed to a block matcher MAY begin at a
      CONTAINER'S CONTENT rather than at the document, re-based so that index 0
      is that first line, and `position` indexes the array given rather than the
      document. A matcher whose answer depends on where it sits in the whole
      document, or on lines the array does not contain, is NOT CONFORMING.

   R2 FOOTNOTES (rendering in PART 9 §16). Footnote labels use the same
      space-run-normalized, case-sensitive key as R1. A [^label] use
      with a matching footnoteDefs entry is numbered by FIRST-REFERENCE order from
      footnoteSeq; repeat references reuse the number (k-th repeat gets
      refId fnref{n}-{k} and an extra backlink). An inline ^[content] note
      draws a fresh anonymous number from the SAME footnoteSeq (it cannot
      be re-referenced). If definitions normalize to one key, the FIRST
      footnote definition wins and every later collision receives the same
      raw-spellings-and-key diagnostic required by R1.
      Unresolved reference -> the literal `[^label]`, a trailing attribute
      run consumed;
      unreferenced definition -> dropped.
      A NOTE INSIDE AN UNRESOLVED REFERENCE IS NOT A REFERENCE -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P9R-004].
      R1 degrades an unresolved reference to its literal
      SOURCE, so the link text that was rendered for it is discarded rather
      than written into the document. A [^label] use or an ^[content] note
      sitting in that text therefore references nothing: it draws no number
      from footnoteSeq, a definition it was the only use of stays
      unreferenced and is dropped by the sentence above, and no endnotes
      section is written on its account.
      Footnotes are numbered only after the enclosing reference's resolution
      outcome is known; pipeline pass order does not change that answer.
      WHAT IS COUNTED IS WHAT THE OUTPUT HOLDS, which decides the cases
      either side of this one the same way. A note in a reference that DOES
      resolve is an ordinary reference, because the resolved link text is
      written (PART 9 §16; corpus
      311-a-footnote-in-reference-link-text-nests-the-anchors-too). A note in
      a bracketed run that never carried a tail is an ordinary reference too,
      because PART 9 §14 renders that run's content. The rule is not about
      brackets; it is about whether the text reached the reader.
   R3 ABBREVIATIONS. Each abbrDefs term matches in rendered text at WORD
      BOUNDARIES only and emits <abbr>. Literal source text emitted for an
      unresolved link or image reference (R1) is not scanned for abbreviations;
      ordinary bracket text remains eligible. The text of a code span, an
      inline literal and a mention is not scanned (carve#4560).
      Expansion follows PART 9 §25 RESOURCE BOUNDS.
   R4 CROSSREFS (behavior detail in PART 9 §19). </#id> matches the
      case-preserved headingIds EXACTLY (neither a case-only nor an ASCII
      spelling of an id resolves; corpus 15-heading-ids), then captionIds for numbered captions and
      directly numbered display equations. Two headings with one explicit id
      both keep it, and the reference resolves to the first in document
      order, quote headings included (carve#4450): unlike R1's heading index,
      a crossref does not decline a heading in a blockquote. The link text is LABEL + NUMBER,
      e.g. "Figure 1" or "Eq. 2"; caption markup is preserved, while a direct
      equation's label is a string. With an empty label the link text is the
      number alone.
      Resolution is ONE LEVEL: cloned heading text is not re-expanded, so a
      self-reference (`# A </#A>`) and a mutual cycle (`# A </#B>` +
      `# B </#A>`) each resolve once (corpus "Cyclic cross-reference resolves
      to one level"). Cloning keeps the heading's markup:
      `# *Setup*` yields `<a href="#…"><strong>Setup</strong></a>`. A clone
      drops every id at every depth, so no id appears twice: with `# *A*{#z}`
      the heading keeps `<strong id="z">`, and `</#A>` gives
      `<a href="#A"><strong>A</strong></a>`. Every other attribute, a title
      included, stays on its own element as written. A footnote
      reference is not cloned: `# Head[^1]` yields `Head`. A `</#id>`
      to an id that is neither a heading nor a numbered target is unresolved
      and renders literally.
      WHAT IS CLONED IS THE HEADING'S INLINE NODES -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P9R-005].
      "Cloned heading text" means the nodes, not a rendered string. A node
      carries its SOURCE RUN, and a string does not, so an implementation that
      flattens the heading to glyphs while building its id index has destroyed
      the run before any renderer is invoked, and no renderer change reaches
      the loss. The same argument covers every other run a renderer may want
      back: a code span, an escape, an inline literal.
      DERIVED DISPLAY TEXT CLONES THE SAME NODES -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P9R-006]. The clause
      above binds every consumer that derives display text from a heading, not
      the crossref alone: a numbered cross-reference label, an index term's
      display, a table-of-contents entry. Flattening to a string at the
      derivation site destroys the source run exactly as flattening at the
      index site does. Stated once here rather than per consumer.
      THE LABEL IS TAKEN BEFORE ANY RENDER-STAGE INJECTION. A heading's
      cloned nodes are its AUTHORED inline content. The `number` that R5a adds
      to display math in a heading is not cloned, so derived display text shows
      the formula without that number. Whatever a render-stage
      transform later adds to the heading -- a `section-number` span, a
      permalink anchor -- is not part of the label and never appears in
      derived text, so an implementation that resolves cross-references AFTER
      such a transform must clone from the PRISTINE heading and not from the
      live one. That is normative rather than incidental because two
      implementations may differ HERE BY CONSTRUCTION: one resolves
      cross-references before the numbering transform runs, the other after
      it, and both follow the same words unless the words say which side of
      the injection the label comes from.
      NUMBERING, PREFIXING AND JOINING REMAIN THE EXTENSION'S OWN
      BUSINESS. This governs what the TITLE part is made of, not the label
      word, not the number, and not the separator around them.
   R5 NUMBERED CAPTIONS. Per bucket key, in document order, each caption whose
      top-level text carries a bare `#` placeholder receives the next
      number for the label's plain-text bucket key (PART 2, CAPTION NUMBER
      PLACEHOLDER); an {#id} on
      the captioned host registers it in captionIds for R4. Only the host's
      block id registers: an id glued to the image or math span inside it is
      an inline attribute and does not, so `![a](/i){#i}` over `^ Fig #`
      leaves `</#i>` literal. For every captionIds
      collision, including collisions between two captions or between a
      caption and an equation, the first registration in document order wins;
      the later target remains numbered but does not replace it.
   R5a LABELED DISPLAY EQUATIONS ARE NUMBERED -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P9R-009].
      A `math` node carrying `display: true` and an authored `label` draws the
      next number in that label's caption bucket. The label has no leading or
      trailing whitespace, so its value is the same plain-text key that PART 2
      derives from a caption label, and it uses R5's document-order counter.
      `label: "Equation"` therefore interleaves with
      captions whose label is `Equation`; `Eq.` and labels in other languages
      remain independent just as they do for captions. Resolution publishes the
      integer as `math.number` beside the authored `label`.

      The node's `attrs.id`, when present, registers the label and number in
      captionIds for R4 with kind `equation`. `label` supplies visible text and
      is NOT the id. Keys are NFC-normalized and matched exactly, as R4
      matches the other id tables. R5's first-registration rule resolves
      collisions within captionIds. R4's heading-first lookup still makes a
      collision with a heading id resolve to the heading.
      Inline math never draws a number, even if an ingested node carries a
      label. `math.label` has no source spelling: a `label` key on a math span
      in Carve source stays an ordinary attribute and draws no number, so R5a
      and CARVE-P12-051 apply to ingested nodes.

      A display-math node at any depth in a `figure` target or a `figure_group`
      draws no direct number when that figure or group's caption carries R5's
      number placeholder. For nested figures and groups, the nearest enclosing
      numbered figure or group owns the node. One numbered caption suppresses
      direct numbering for every display-math descendant in its target or
      children. The equations keep their authored labels, but
      their `number` fields stay absent and their own `attrs.id` values do not
      register in captionIds. A reference to such an equation id therefore
      remains unresolved unless the numbered caption independently registers
      that same id. A caption without the placeholder owns no number and does
      not suppress R5a.
   R6 SECTION WRAPPING consumes headingIds after dedup -- the stateful
      emission algorithm in PART 9 §13.
   R7 BLOCK IMAGE PROMOTION -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P9R-007]. Block-image status is a
      property of the RESOLVED tree, never of the source line. `![a][r]` is a
      block image where `[r]: /u` is written and ordinary paragraph text where
      it is not, and the definition may sit anywhere in the document -- so the
      question cannot be answered while the block layer is still reading lines.

      ONE PHASE, AFTER R1. A paragraph whose whole content resolves to a single
      image becomes a BLOCK IMAGE (PART 9 §4's image-paragraph host,
      CARVE-P11-004's writer-side collapse, PART 10's bare `<img>`). A construct
      that renders nothing does not count as content. This is the ONLY place the
      question is asked. Nothing before it may treat an image
      paragraph as a block image, and nothing after it may re-derive the answer
      -- a renderer, a writer and an importer all READ it (PART 12 §23 names
      the field they read it from).

      THE CAPTION BINDS HERE, NOT SYNTACTICALLY. A `^ ` line after an image
      paragraph is an UNBOUND SLOT until this phase runs: it is not a caption,
      and it is not paragraph text. The phase binds it as the block image's
      caption where the paragraph is promoted, and where it is NOT promoted the
      slot's SOURCE LINES -- all of them, a multi-line caption included -- go
      back to the paragraph as ordinary text. So an unresolved reference image
      does not merely leave a paragraph behind; it takes the caption line into
      it.

      That binding is resolution-dependent BY DESIGN. Binding on the SOURCE
      SHAPE would put a `<figure>` around a paragraph of literal `![a][r]` --
      output no engine writes and no clause asks for.

      A DISPLAY-MATH HOST HAS NOTHING TO WAIT FOR. PART 9 §4's other prose-spelled
      captionable host, a paragraph whose whole content is one display-math
      span, is captionable and is not a block image: it has no reference to
      resolve, so its slot binds outright. The syntactic filter that decides
      WHICH paragraphs carry a slot at all is shared between the two hosts and
      settles nothing else -- an unresolved reference image is
      captionable-SHAPED, and it is not a block image.

   ============================================================================ *)
